index 0000000..9fc41f6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/dotfiles/notmuch/spec.org
@@ -0,0 +1,174 @@
+#+TITLE: Pat's mail client on beastie: read and send through lab-mail
+
+artifact: the mail client config in =dotfiles/notmuch= and
+=dotfiles/emacs=, the packages it needs in
+=infra/hosts/beastie/ports=, and one allowed sender in lab-mail's
+smtprelay config.
+
+goal: Pat reads the list and sends mail from notmuch-emacs on beastie
+through lab-mail, as pat@p7x.dev. beastie holds no mail credential;
+lab-mail holds the only one. See =jails/instances/lab-mail/spec.org=.
+
+provider: set up by the operator on Fastmail, outside the repo.
+- pat@p7x.dev is a sending identity on the Fastmail account behind
+  lab-mail's =mail-creds=, as claude@p7x.dev is.
+- Mail sent to the list, lab-devel@p7x.dev, is filed into the folder
+  =lists= by the rule that files all list mail there.
+
+* beastie
+
+packages:
+- =infra/hosts/beastie/ports= includes mail/isync and mail/msmtp.
+
+lab-mail:
+- smtprelay's =allowed_sender= lists pat@p7x.dev and
+  claude@p7x.dev.
+
+profile: the =dotfiles/notmuch= package installs a second notmuch
+profile, =lists=, used with =NOTMUCH_PROFILE=lists=, beside the
+default profile, the jj maildir.
+- Its config is =~/.config/notmuch/lists/config=:
+  - =mail_root= is =~/mail=, and the database path is
+    =~/.local/share/notmuch/lists=, set explicitly so the profile can
+    never index into the default profile's database.
+  - =primary_email= is pat@p7x.dev, and =other_email= is
+    pat@patmaddox.com, so the tag hook counts mail from either as
+    sent.
+  - It adds no tags on =notmuch new=, indexes =Change-Id= as
+    =changeid:=, keeps flag sync off, and ignores mbsync's state
+    files, as the default profile and lab-claude's =lists= profile do.
+- Its hooks are in =~/.config/notmuch/lists/hooks=, notmuch's hook dir
+  for the profile.
+  - post-new runs the same =mail-tag.sh= as the default profile, so
+    sent, inbox and replied are derived the same way.
+  - pre-new runs =mbsync lists= and then removes deleted drafts, as
+    the default pre-new does. A =notmuch new= in the profile, or a
+    refresh in Emacs, fetches and indexes in one step.
+  - If mbsync fails, pre-new exits non-zero and =notmuch new= stops
+    with mbsync's error, so a fetch that failed is never silent.
+- The package installs the config like the default one: only if none
+  exists, and removed on deinstall only if unedited.
+
+fetch:
+- =~/.mbsyncrc= pulls the =lists= mailbox from lab-mail,
+  127.0.0.1:2143, into =~/mail/lists=. It is pull-only: no expunge,
+  no remove, and no flags pushed. lab-mail accepts any login, so the
+  user and password in it are placeholders, as in lab-claude's.
+
+send:
+- =~/.msmtprc= sends through lab-mail, 127.0.0.1:2525, with no auth
+  and no TLS, from pat@p7x.dev. It holds no password.
+- msmtp sends without a queue. When lab-mail refuses an address, or
+  Fastmail refuses the message or cannot be reached, msmtp exits
+  non-zero with the server's reply, and the message goes to no one.
+
+dotfiles: the package installs =~/.mbsyncrc= and =~/.msmtprc= the way
+it installs the notmuch config: only if absent, and removed on
+deinstall only if unedited. A home that already has either file keeps
+it, and the operator merges the account in by hand.
+
+* emacs
+
+read:
+- Emacs sets =NOTMUCH_PROFILE= to =lists= at startup, so
+  notmuch-emacs, and every notmuch it runs, use the =lists= profile.
+- Refreshing in Emacs runs =notmuch new=, which fetches through the
+  profile's pre-new hook.
+
+send:
+- message-mode sends with msmtp as its sendmail program. The envelope
+  sender is the From header.
+- From is pat@p7x.dev, the profile's =primary_email=.
+- A failed send signals an error with msmtp's output and keeps the
+  compose buffer, so the message is not lost and can be sent again.
+
+sent copies:
+- Each sent message is filed with Fcc into =~/mail/sent= and tagged
+  sent at once, so it shows in its thread without waiting for the
+  list to bring it back.
+- =~/mail/sent= is beside =~/mail/lists=, not in it. mbsync owns
+  =~/mail/lists=.
+- When the list copy arrives, notmuch indexes both files under one
+  Message-ID, so the thread shows the message once.
+- The Fcc copy only lowers latency. The tags come from the list's
+  contents on each =notmuch new=, so a database rebuilt from
+  =~/mail/lists= alone gets the same sent, inbox and replied tags.
+
+drafts: drafts go in =~/mail/drafts=, notmuch-emacs's default folder
+under the profile's =mail_root=. The tag hook already leaves drafts
+untagged, and pre-new removes deleted ones.
+
+jj maildir: Emacs does not refer to the jj maildir. =dotfiles/emacs=
+has no =maildir-deliver.sh= and no settings that point at it.
+
+* acceptance test
+
+Run on beastie as Pat, with lab-mail running.
+1. A message sent to lab-devel@p7x.dev shows up in Emacs within two
+   minutes of sending, after a refresh.
+2. A reply to it from Emacs is sent with From pat@p7x.dev, and
+   msmtp exits 0. Its copy is in =~/mail/sent= and tagged sent before
+   the next refresh.
+3. After the list brings the reply back and Emacs refreshes, the
+   thread shows the reply once, and the message it answers is tagged
+   replied.
+4. A message to someone@example.com fails to send. Emacs shows
+   smtprelay's refusal and keeps the compose buffer.
+5. With lab-mail stopped, sending fails and keeps the buffer, and a
+   refresh reports mbsync's error.
+6. No file in Pat's home holds a mail password: =~/.msmtprc= has
+   =auth off=, and =~/.mbsyncrc= has only the placeholder.
+7. After removing =~/.local/share/notmuch/lists= and running
+   =NOTMUCH_PROFILE=lists notmuch new=, every message has the same
+   sent, inbox and replied tags as before.
+
+* out of scope
+
+- Moving the agent's process-email workflow off the jj maildir. It is
+  a separate spec, and this one assumes it: mail Pat sends over SMTP
+  never reaches the jj maildir, so an agent that reads only the jj
+  maildir does not see it.
+- Removing =libexec/mail/= and the default profile in
+  =dotfiles/notmuch=. They serve the agent's workflow, and its spec
+  decides what becomes of them.
+- Sending as pat@patmaddox.com. It takes a Fastmail identity on the
+  account behind =mail-creds= and one more allowed sender, and
+  nothing in this spec depends on the address.
+- Restricting who on beastie can send as pat@p7x.dev. Every process on
+  beastie and in its =ip4 = inherit= jails can reach smtprelay, so
+  any of them can send as any allowed sender. That is accepted for
+  now; per-user auth on smtprelay or a separate address for lab-claude
+  would close it later.
+- git send-email from beastie. =libexec/mail/= delivers series into
+  the jj maildir.
+
+* alternatives considered
+
+- pat@p7x.dev through lab-mail, not pat@patmaddox.com with its own
+  app password. pat@patmaddox.com is Pat's primary account, and any
+  credential for it on beastie, even an app password in pass, is one
+  more secret on disk. pat@p7x.dev is already a Fastmail identity on
+  the relay's account, and lab-mail already holds that credential.
+- A second profile, not replacing the default one. The default
+  profile serves the agent's workflow, and Emacs chooses its profile
+  with one setting, so going back to the jj maildir is one change.
+- Fetch from the profile's pre-new hook, not on a timer. Mail is
+  fetched when Pat looks, with no daemon or cron entry on beastie, and
+  an Emacs refresh both fetches and indexes. A timer can be added
+  later if waiting for the fetch on refresh is too slow.
+- msmtp, not Emacs's built-in smtpmail. msmtp is what lab-claude uses
+  against the same relay, so both clients behave and fail the same
+  way, and git send-email can use the same account later. smtpmail
+  needs no package, but it is one more client config to keep in step.
+- No queue in the client (msmtpq), for the reason the lab-mail spec
+  gives: a queue answers "sent" before Fastmail has the message, and a
+  failed send in Emacs keeps the compose buffer.
+- Fcc into =~/mail/sent=, not into =~/mail/lists=, and not no Fcc. A
+  file mbsync did not write in its maildir is a state mbsync does not
+  expect. Without an Fcc, a sent message is missing from its thread
+  until the next fetch after the list delivers it.
+- The dotfiles as package samples, not files the package owns. A home
+  may already have its own =~/.mbsyncrc= or =~/.msmtprc= with other
+  accounts, and the package must not overwrite them.
+- Profile name =lists=, not a new one. lab-claude's profile for the
+  same mailbox has that name, so one command works in both places.

